  "narrative": {
    "text": "**Business & market dynamics:** Owlet’s core smart-monitor business posted continued revenue growth and record subscription sales, though momentum slowed from prior-year pace. Gross margin expanded despite tariff headwinds, reflecting improved product mix, yet the company swung to a net loss from a prior-year profit. The high-margin Owlet 360 subscription app and BabySat’s planned healthcare entry, along with international expansion, form a multi-pronged growth narrative. However, the recent sharp stock selloff and margin pressure point to a market that remains cautious about near-term profitability and the durability of demand.\n\n**Management strategy and execution:** Founder Kurt Workman has resumed the CEO role, signaling a strategic refocus on the company’s original mission. Prior leadership was marked by equity dilution, soft forward guidance, and margin stress that hurt credibility and triggered a steep share-price decline. Current execution emphasizes subscription adoption and healthcare-market development, but confidence is low until management demonstrates it can convert these catalysts into consistent profitability without further dilutive capital actions.",
